THE DIAMOND PROBLEM — why multiple class inheritance is dangerous:

```java
// ═══ HYPOTHETICAL: if Java allowed multiple class inheritance ═══

class Vehicle {
    public void start() { System.out.println("Vehicle starting"); }
}

class ElectricVehicle extends Vehicle {
    @Override
    public void start() { System.out.println("Electric motor starting silently"); }
}

class GasVehicle extends Vehicle {
    @Override
    public void start() { System.out.println("Gas engine starting with roar"); }
}

// ❌ ILLEGAL in Java — but IF it were allowed:
// class HybridVehicle extends ElectricVehicle, GasVehicle { }
//
// HybridVehicle h = new HybridVehicle();
// h.start();  // WHICH start()? Electric's silent? Gas's roar? AMBIGUOUS!
//
// This is the DIAMOND PROBLEM:
//        Vehicle
//       /      \
// Electric    Gas
//       \      /
//       Hybrid    ← which start() does Hybrid inherit?
```

JAVA'S SOLUTION — multiple interface implementation:

```java
// ═══ INTERFACES: no ambiguity because class provides ALL implementations ═══

public interface Tradable {
    void executeTrade();  // abstract — class MUST implement

    default String getMarket() { return "NSE"; }
}

public interface Reportable {
    void generateReport();  // abstract — class MUST implement

    default String getMarket() { return "BSE"; }  // SAME default method signature as Tradable!
}

// Class implements BOTH:
public class TradingService implements Tradable, Reportable {

    @Override
    public void executeTrade() { System.out.println("Trade executed"); }

    @Override
    public void generateReport() { System.out.println("Report generated"); }

    // ═══ CONFLICT: both interfaces have getMarket() default ═══
    // COMPILE ERROR if not overridden: "class inherits unrelated defaults for getMarket()"
    @Override
    public String getMarket() {
        // Class MUST resolve explicitly — three options:
        return Tradable.super.getMarket();     // Option 1: delegate to Tradable's version
        // return Reportable.super.getMarket();  // Option 2: delegate to Reportable's version
        // return "NSE + BSE";                    // Option 3: own implementation
    }
}
// NO ambiguity! The CLASS decides. Compiler forces the resolution.
```

THREE resolution strategies for conflicting default methods:

```java
// ═══ STRATEGY 1: delegate to ONE interface's default ═══
@Override
public String getMarket() {
    return Tradable.super.getMarket();  // "NSE"
    // InterfaceName.super.method() — calls specific interface's default
}

// ═══ STRATEGY 2: combine BOTH ═══
@Override
public String getMarket() {
    return Tradable.super.getMarket() + " + " + Reportable.super.getMarket();  // "NSE + BSE"
}

// ═══ STRATEGY 3: own implementation ═══
@Override
public String getMarket() {
    return "NSE (primary), BSE (secondary)";
}
```

WHY interfaces avoid the diamond problem:

```java
// 1. Abstract methods: NO body → no ambiguity (class provides the body)
// 2. Default methods: if conflict → COMPILE ERROR forces class to resolve
// 3. Static methods: NOT inherited → no conflict possible
// 4. Class always wins: if class has the method, it overrides ALL interface defaults

// Rule: class method > sub-interface default > super-interface default
```

The class-wins rule:

```java
public interface Auditable {
    default String audit() { return "Interface audit"; }
}

public class BaseService {
    public String audit() { return "Class audit"; }
}

// BaseService has audit(). Auditable has default audit().
public class TradingService extends BaseService implements Auditable {
    // NO override needed! BaseService.audit() WINS over Auditable.audit()
    // Because: class method > interface default method
}

TradingService ts = new TradingService();
ts.audit();  // "Class audit" — class wins!
```

